.Sh DESCRIPTION
The library authenticates exact policy bytes in a non-allocated
.Dv SHT_NOTE
section named
.Pa .note.5bsd.signature ,
outside all ELF program segments.
A redundant footer must agree with the note locator.
An unsigned non-allocated .5bsd.policy section
is accepted only as build input to the signer.
It does not parse UCL or grant attributes.
Verification returns the policy bytes consumed by the authenticated hash.
The caller owns that allocation and releases it with
.Fn capimage_policy_free .
.Pp
Images use Ed25519 signatures over a domain-separated SHA-256 digest of every
file byte, substituting zeros only for the fixed 64-byte signature field.
The signature covers ELF headers, interpreter and dependency information,
initial data, the public key, the policy and non-loadable bytes.
Both ELF classes and byte orders are supported.
Duplicate build policy sections and malformed bounds are rejected.
.Pp
A non-NULL public key explicitly selects a PEM Ed25519 verification key.
A NULL public key selects the system catalogue
.Pa /etc/capability/keys .
Each catalogue file is named by the 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits of the
raw public key followed by
.Pa .pub .
The directory and key files must be root-owned and not group/other writable;
key files must be regular files and symbolic links are refused.
Installing a key in this catalogue is an administrative trust decision.
.Pp
Detached seals authenticate an entire regular file with a caller-selected
protocol domain.
Different artifact protocols must use different domains.
.Pp
.Fn capimage_embed
accepts a private writable copy of a linked ELF image.
It appends the signature note, authenticated original-header record, copied
section metadata and a fixed little-endian footer.
Only the ELF section-table locator fields change in the original header;
program-header and load-segment offsets remain unchanged.
All new metadata is included in whole-file signing.
If a policy is already present, the supplied bytes must match it exactly.
An existing unsigned build-input section is retained in the original ELF
prefix, but is not an alternate source of authenticated runtime policy.
The caller must sign the result before publication.
.Pp
.Fn capimage_remove
restores the authenticated original ELF header and truncates a private
writable copy to its recorded original length.
Signature removal verifies integrity before using the restoration metadata.
An unsigned build-input section, if originally present, is retained.
No signing key is required, and the result grants no authority.
.Pp
.Fn capimage_elf_info_fd
returns structural ELF signature metadata, including the original length and
header needed for verified signature removal.
Unsigned inputs retain their full original length.
Malformed signature containers are rejected.
This operation does not establish integrity or publisher trust.
.Pp
The system trust catalogue and enforcement marker are accessed through
root-owned directory descriptors for /etc and /etc/capability, and the keys
subdirectory when verifying a key.
Symlinks and group/other writable ancestors are rejected.
.Pp
.Fn capimage_verify_integrity
and
.Fn capimage_check_seal_integrity
check the signature against its embedded public key without consulting the
system trust catalogue.
They reject unsigned input and invalid signatures.
They are intended for personal software whose signing key need not be
administratively enrolled.
Success proves integrity under the embedded key, not a trusted publisher,
platform status, or permission to use privileged attributes.
The image API returns the same authenticated policy representation as
.Fn capimage_verify ;
the caller must free it and independently confine the program's authority.
.Ss Explicit pinned integrity handles
.Fn capimage_prepare_fd
requests a kernel-owned text pin before verifying a recognized signed image.
On success it returns 0 and supplies a close-on-exec handle and fixed-size
integrity metadata.
An optional unsigned image returns 0 with
.Fa handle
set to -1 and zeroed metadata, without acquiring a persistent pin.
.Dv CAPIMAGE_PREPARE_REQUIRED
instead rejects a missing signature with
.Er ENOATTR .
.Dv CAPIMAGE_PREPARE_RAW_DIGEST
also requests the raw whole-file SHA-256 digest used by bundle seals.
That digest differs from the domain-separated image digest whose signature
field is replaced by zeros.
.Dv CAPIMAGE_PREPARE_VVERIFY
preserves the current veriexec fingerprint enrollment checks of
.Dv O_VERIFY .
.Pp
.Fn capimage_handle_policy
reads authenticated policy bytes through the pinned handle.
It checks the kernel descriptor type through the MAC policy syscall and
validates the returned policy digest.
No policy-sized copy is retained in kernel memory.
Close the handle with
.Xr close 2 ;
duplicated or inherited references keep the pin until the final close.
Keep a reference until the intended consumer has pinned the same original
executable descriptor; reopening its pathname is not an identity handoff.
.Pp
These interfaces establish integrity only.
They do not enroll a signer, confer platform authority, or replace current
execution permission and MAC checks.
The existing verification interfaces retain their behavior.
There is no implicit fallback if the new operation or filesystem is unsupported,
or if verification fails.
Reusable handles currently support local UFS and ZFS backends;
tmpfs and other filesystems return
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
for recognized signed images.
Tmpfs is excluded because its vnode text pin does not exclude existing
writable shared mappings; no mutable verified handle is returned.
Unsigned inspection still returns no handle without acquiring a text pin,
including when writable descriptors or mappings exist.
Ordinary unsigned execution is unchanged.
.Pp
.Fn capimage_prepare_trusted_fd
adds the existing signer enrollment check to
.Fn capimage_prepare_fd .
A null key selects the protected system catalogue; an explicit key selects
that caller-defined trust policy.
Personal integrity-only consumers use
.Fn capimage_prepare_fd
instead.
Trust failure closes the temporary handle and returns
.Er EAUTH .
The function never falls back to one-shot verification.
Signer enrollment does not itself authorize restricted policy attributes.
.Pp
The current MAC syscall gate rejects handle preparation and metadata operations
with
.Er ECAPMODE
once the caller has entered Capsicum mode.
The experimental consumer is supervisor-only; positioned-read rights checks
do not imply that entered capability mode is supported.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The functions return zero on success and -1 on failure, setting errno and
an optional bounded error message.
A missing policy returns ENOENT, an unsigned build-input section returns
ENOATTR, malformed structure returns EFTYPE,
and an invalid or untrusted signature returns EAUTH.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr capsign 8 ,
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The one-shot verification interfaces are not content locks.
Explicit image handles retain a content pin for their lifetime.
Runtime callers must retain an immutable snapshot or a kernel content pin
from before verification until the verified object is used.
A pathname, inode number, advisory lock or successful signature check alone
does not provide this lifetime guarantee.
.Pp
An authentic signature identifies a signer; it does not authorize that signer
to claim every attribute.
The policy consumer must enforce signer authority and library integrity.
Never parse unauthenticated policy as authority.
.Pp
Preparing a handle requires
.Dv CAP_PREAD
rights on the source descriptor and does not change its file offset.
A descriptor restricted to sequential reads cannot be used to obtain a
handle exposing arbitrary policy offsets.
